<h1>Spreader Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A spreader conveyor distributes products evenly across the full belt width from a concentrated infeed stream for uniform downstream processing. Products arriving in narrow lanes, clumps, or piles spread into a single, even layer before entering freezing tunnels, drying conveyors, coating lines, or <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">cooling conveyors</a>. Spreader types include vibratory spreaders (oscillation-driven lateral distribution), diverging belt spreaders (angled belt sections push product outward), and mechanical plow spreaders (angled deflectors redistribute flow). Spreaders are most common in food processing, where freezers, dryers, sorters, and coaters all depend on an even layer.</p>
<h2 id="why-heading">Why Even Distribution Matters</h2>
<p>Processing equipment, freezers, dryers, coaters, and coolers, applies treatment uniformly across the belt width. If product distribution is uneven (concentrated in the center, thin on the edges), the treatment is uneven: center products receive insufficient treatment (under-frozen, under-dried), edge products receive excessive treatment (over-frozen, over-dried). This creates quality variation, waste, and food safety risks.</p>
<p>Specific consequences of uneven distribution:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Freezing:</strong> Clumped products freeze slowly in the center and can leave the freezer under-frozen. Products frozen together (clumping) require mechanical separation before packaging, causing breakage. IQF (Individually Quick Frozen) quality requires single-layer, evenly spaced product.</li>
<li><strong>Drying:</strong> Over-loaded belt sections dry slowly. Under-loaded sections over-dry. Moisture content variation causes shelf-life variation in the same production batch.</li>
<li><strong>Coating:</strong> Chocolate enrobing, seasoning application, and breading systems deposit coating evenly across the belt. Product clumps receive coating only on exposed surfaces, the sides touching other products remain uncoated.</li>
<li><strong>Cooling:</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">Cooling conveyor</a> airflow distributes uniformly. Dense product sections block airflow and cool slowly. Thin sections waste cooling capacity.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="types-heading">Spreader Conveyor Types</h2>
<h3>Vibratory Spreaders</h3>
<p>Vibratory spreaders use controlled oscillation to move and distribute products laterally across the belt or tray surface. Electromagnetic drives usually run at a fixed frequency tied to line power with adjustable stroke, while mechanical eccentric-weight drives run at lower frequency with a longer stroke. On its vibratory screeners, for example, <a href="https://www.eriez.com/Products/Material-Handling/Vibratory-Feeders-Conveyors/Vibratory-Screeners" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Eriez lists</a> 3,600 vibrations per minute and a 0.060 in (1.5 mm) stroke for electromagnetic models and 900 to 1,200 cycles per minute with up to 7/16 in (11 mm) stroke for mechanical models. Product migrates from concentrated infeed areas toward the edges, spreading across the full width.</p>
<p>Vibratory spreaders handle small, free-flowing products: nuts, candies, shrimp, diced vegetables, cereal pieces, and IQF fruits. Operators tune spreading by adjusting stroke on electromagnetic drives or motor speed (often by VFD) on mechanical drives, plus pan slope and any spreading fins or dividers.</p>
<h3>Diverging Belt Spreaders</h3>
<p>Diverging belt spreaders use two or more belt sections angled outward from the center. Product arriving at the center of the infeed rides onto the angled belts, which carry it laterally toward the edges. The angle and belt speed determine the rate of spreading. Multiple diverging stages achieve progressively wider distribution.</p>
<p>Angled-roller modular belts such as Intralox's <a href="/conveyor-types/activated-roller-belt/">Activated Roller Belt (ARB)</a> move product sideways while it travels forward, without separate belt sections. The roller angle sets the direction of lateral travel. <a href="https://www.intralox.com/products/arb-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intralox lists</a> aligning, gapping, merging, singulating, and sorting among ARB functions. Spreading is the same lateral-motion principle run outward, so ask the supplier to test it on your product before relying on it.</p>
<h3>Mechanical Plow Spreaders</h3>
<p>Plow spreaders use angled deflector plates (plows) mounted above or on the conveyor surface. Product flow contacts the plow surface and redirects laterally. Multiple plows at progressively wider angles achieve full-width distribution. Plows are simple, have no drive, and suit robust products that tolerate contact with the deflector surface. Not suitable for fragile products that break on impact.</p>
<h3>Oscillating Conveyors</h3>
<p>Oscillating (reciprocating) conveyors use a belt or tray that sweeps back and forth laterally while product feeds continuously from above. The sweeping motion distributes product in a uniform layer across the downstream belt. Common at the discharge point of <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a>, screw conveyors, and hoppers that deliver product in a concentrated stream.</p>
