<h2 id="apps-heading">Spreader Conveyor Applications</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>IQF freezing:</strong> Spreader conveyors before IQF tunnels ensure single-layer product distribution. Freezer suppliers often supply the infeed spreader with the freezer. Even distribution prevents product clumping and ensures consistent freezing rates.</li>
<li><strong>Drying:</strong> Dehydrators and industrial dryers process product evenly across the belt width. Spreaders upstream of the dryer equalize product depth for consistent moisture removal.</li>
<li><strong>Coating and seasoning:</strong> Enrobers, breading machines, and seasoning applicators apply coatings uniformly. Spreaders ensure single-layer product presentation for full coating coverage.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">Cooling</a>:</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">Bakery</a> and confectionery cooling conveyors achieve uniform cooling when product distributes evenly across the mesh belt.</li>
<li><strong>Optical sorting:</strong> Vision-based optical sorters require single-layer product on the inspection belt. Spreaders upstream achieve the single-layer requirement.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection:</strong> X-ray and metal detection systems perform best with single-layer product. Clumped or stacked product creates false rejects or missed contaminants. See <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject systems</a> for more on inspection conveyors.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Spreader Conveyor</h2>
<p>Spreader selection depends on product characteristics, throughput, and distribution quality requirements:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Product size and fragility:</strong> Small, robust products (nuts, candies) suit vibratory spreaders. Larger or fragile products (fillets, baked goods) need diverging belt or oscillating spreaders. Fragile products cannot tolerate mechanical plow contact.</li>
<li><strong>Throughput:</strong> High-throughput lines need wider belts and more aggressive spreading. Vibratory spreaders scale with belt width. Diverging belt spreaders handle higher volume per foot of width.</li>
<li><strong>Distribution uniformity:</strong> IQF freezing and optical sorting demand near-perfect single-layer distribution. Drying and cooling tolerate moderate variation. Match spreader quality to downstream process sensitivity.</li>
<li><strong>Sanitary requirements:</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Food-grade spreaders</a> use stainless steel construction, FDA-compliant belt materials, and designs that facilitate washdown cleaning.</li>
<li><strong>Infeed pattern:</strong> Single-lane infeed requires more lateral spreading than wide-lane infeed. Point-source discharge (from a hopper or elevator) needs the most aggressive spreading.</li>
</ol>
<p>For budgeting, simple plow deflectors cost least because they have no drive. Vibratory and diverging belt systems cost more because of drives, controls, and sanitary construction. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for general cost factors.</p>
